'''Nunakuma Shrine''' (沼名前神社) is a [[Shinto shrine]] located in [[Fukuyama, Hiroshima|Fukuyama City]], [[Hiroshima Prefecture|Hiroshima]] Prefecture [[Japan]]. It is a [[Gion shrine]].<ref name="fukuyamatomonoura-2020a">{{Cite web |last=fukuyama_tomonoura |date=2020-01-28 |title="Nunakuma Shrine" a little Gion protecting the town and the sea of Tomonoura {{!}} VISIT鞆の浦 |url=https://visittomonoura.com/2020/01/4111/?lang=en |access-date=2023-12-02 |language=en-US}}</ref> It is a [[Beppyo shrine]], or a shrine that is particularly notable in a certain way with a significant history to it.<ref>{{Cite web |title=別表神社とは？御朱印めぐりに参考になる「別表神社一覧」とマップ {{!}} 開運戦隊ゴシュインジャー |url=https://jinja-gosyuin.com/beppyou/ |access-date=2023-04-02 |website=jinja-gosyuin.com}}</ref> It is located in [[Fukuyama, Hiroshima]].

It has two component shrines  {{Nihongo|Watasu Shrine|渡守神社|Watasu Jinja}} and {{Nihongo|Tomo Gion Shrine|鞆祇園宮|Tomo Gion-gu}}<ref>{{Cite web |last=a-tsukamoto |date=2021-02-24 |title=Shrine ritual of the Bow {{!}} VISIT鞆の浦 |url=https://visittomonoura.com/oyumi-shinji/?lang=en |access-date=2023-12-07 |language=en-US}}</ref> which were merged together due to the [[Shrine Consolidation Policy]].[[File:Nunakuma-jinja_ninotorii-2.jpg|right|thumb|220x220px|Main Torii gate]]
It has a famous stone gate and [[Noh]] stage that were donated by the [[Mizuno family]] retainers of the [[Fukuyama Domain]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Nunakuma Shrine（Hiroshima） |url=https://www.jldb.bunka.go.jp/en/location/ |access-date=2023-12-07 |website=Nationwide location database |language=en}}</ref><ref name="fukuyamatomonoura-2020a" />

== Enshrined deities ==
Two deities are enshrined at the shrine.

; Main enshrined deity
:* [[Watatsumi|Owatatsumi]] no Mikoto - The enshrined deity of the old Watasu Shrine.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=124}}
; Secondary enshrined deity
:* [[Susanoo-no-Mikoto]] - see [[Gion worship]] for more info<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=124}}

== History ==
The shrine is ancient. It was originally two shrines, and both are mentioned in the [[Engishiki Jinmyocho]] written in 927.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=124}} Its area was very strategically important on the [[Seto Inland Sea]]..<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=124}}

In 1711 a Korean emissary said it was the most beautiful place in Japan, even more than [[Itsukushima Shrine]].<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=124}}

In the Meiji era, {{Nihongo|Watasu Shrine|渡守神社|Watasu Jinja}} and {{Nihongo|Tomo Gion Shrine|鞆祇園宮|Tomo Gion-gu}} were combined and renamed as Nunakuma Shrine according to the [[Engishiki|Engishiki Jinmyocho]]. according to the [[Shrine Consolidation Policy]]. The shrine continues to enshrine two deities, [[Watatsumi|Owatatsumi-no-Mikoto]] and [[Susanoo-no-Mikoto]], who were originally enshrined separately by each shrine.<ref name="https-2">https://tomo-gionsan.com/english.pdf</ref>

=== Watasu Shrine ===
According to legend Watasu shrine, was established in the second year of [[Emperor Chūai|Emperor Chuai's]] reign.<ref name="https-2" /> [[Empress Jingū|Empress Jingu]] headed to western Japan and enshrined Watatsumi-no-mikoto on a local sacred stone, praying for safe sea routes after her [[Empress Jingū#Korean Invasion|failed invasion of Korea]]. On her way back, she reportedly said to Watatsumi no Mikoto, "Takatomo Izuno." The name "Tomo" is said to have originated from the fact that "tomo" was one of the tools used for bows.<ref name="https-2" /><ref name=":0">{{Cite journal |last=Faure |first=Bernard |date=2021-01-01 |title=GODS OF MEDIEVAL JAPAN - Vol. 3 |url=https://www.academia.edu/60480771/GODS_OF_MEDIEVAL_JAPAN_Vol_3_Rage_and_Ravage |journal=Rage and Ravage}}</ref>{{Rp|page=124}} Tomo is a wrist protector used when firing arrows.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=124}} She gave hers to the shrine as a gift to thank [[Watatsumi]] for her safe return from Korea.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=124}}

There are 22 Watatsumi shrines in the region that claim to have been founded by her after her return from Korea.<ref name="wwwforgottenbookscom-2023a">{{Cite web |title=The Romance of Kobe |url=https://www.forgottenbooks.com/en/readbook/TheRomanceofKobe_10127101#12 |access-date=2023-11-24 |website=www.forgottenbooks.com}}</ref>{{rp|7}} Most notably [[Watatsumi Shrine (Kobe)|Watatsumi Shrine]] in [[Kobe]].<ref name="wwwforgottenbookscom-2023a" />{{rp|7}}<ref name="shintoshrines-2023b">{{Cite web |title=Watatsumi Shrine {{!}} 海神社 {{!}}Hyogo-ken, Kobe-shi |url=https://www.rodsshinto.com/28-108-01 |access-date=2023-04-06 |website=shintoshrines |language=en}}</ref> <ref name="wwwforgottenbookscom-2023a" />{{rp|7}}

In the [[Engishiki Jinmyocho]], published in 927,<ref>[[Louis-Frédéric|Nussbaum, Louis-Frédéric]]. (2005). [https://books.google.com/books?id=p2QnPijAEmEC&pg=PA178&dq= "''Engi-shiki''"] in ''Japan Encyclopedia'', p. 178.</ref> there is a description of a [[Engishiki Jinmyocho|Shikinaisha]] called 'Numakuma-jinja Shrine' located in Numakuma District, [[Bingo Province]].<ref name="https-2" /><ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=124}} 

In modern times, Watasu Shrine is said to enshrine [[Sarutahiko Ōkami|Sarutahiko-no-kami]] and Funatama-no-mikoto. The shrine was originally located in Gojihira Village or Tomonoseki-cho and was moved to its current location during the [[Keichō|Keicho]] era (1596-1615) as a precinct shrine of Gion Shrine. In the [[Meireki]] era (1655-1658), the main shrine was rebuilt by the feudal lord Katsutada Mizuno.

It has a three day long festival on august 11th of the lunar calendar.<ref>{{Cite web |title=【Tomonoura Events】 - TOMO-MONOGATARI |url=https://tomomonogatari.com/en/ |access-date=2023-12-07 |website=tomomonogatari.com |language=en}}</ref>
=== Tomo Gion Shrine ===
The origins of this subshrine are less clear, but it was extant at the time of 927 when the [[Engishiki Jinmyocho]] was written.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=124}}

It is a [[Gion Shrine]]<ref name="https-2" /> Gion shrines originally worshipped [[Gozu Tenno]], a Buddhist influenced [[Onmyōdō]] deity<ref>{{Cite book |last=Lillehoj |first=Elizabeth |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=FogSFWDe0hUC&pg=PA218 |title=Critical Perspectives on Classicism in Japanese Painting: 1600 - 1700 |date=2004-01-01 |publisher=University of Hawaii Press |isbn=978-0-8248-2699-4 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Japan Shinto Kami Gods {{!}} Gozu-Tennō 牛頭天王{{!}} Rods Shinto |url=https://www.rodsshinto.com/gozu-tenno |access-date=2022-01-10 |website=shintoshrines |language=en}}</ref><ref name=":1">{{Cite journal |last=McMullin |first=Neil |date=1988-02-01 |title=On Placating the Gods and Pacifying the Populace: The Case of the Gion "Goryō" Cult |url=https://www.journals.uchicago.edu/doi/10.1086/463123 |journal=History of Religions |volume=27 |issue=3 |pages=270–293 |doi=10.1086/463123 |issn=0018-2710 |s2cid=162357693}}</ref> that was later syncretized with [[Susanoo-no-Mikoto]].<ref name=":1" />

According to the Engishiki Jinmyocho, this shrine is the shrine is the source by which [[Gozu Tenno]] entered [[Yasaka Shrine]] through [[Kanjo]], this makes it highly significant to the history of the [[Gion cult]].<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=124}}

The shrine is commonly referred to as 'Gion-san' by locals.<ref name="fukuyamatomonoura-2020a" />

Its [[torii]] are listed as [[Important Cultural Property (Japan)|Important Cultural Property]] of Hiroshima prefecture.<ref name="fukuyamatomonoura-2020a" />
