[[File:Nunakuma-jinja ninotorii-2.jpg|thumb|220px|right|{{center|Second torii gate (Hiroshima Prefecture–designated cultural property)}}]]
'''Nunakuma Shrine''' (ぬなくまじんじゃ) is a {{ill|lt=Shinto shrine|Q845945|WD=Q845945|1=Shinto shrine}} located in Tomo-chō Ushiroji, {{ill|lt=Fukuyama City|Q661038|WD=Q661038|1=Fukuyama, Hiroshima}}, {{ill|lt=Hiroshima Prefecture|Q617375|WD=Q617375|1=Hiroshima Prefecture}}. It is a {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Shikinaisha|1=Shikinaisha|WD=Q134917286}}, and under the former {{ill|lt=Modern system of ranked Shinto shrines|Q712534|WD=Q712534|1=Modern system of ranked Shinto shrines}}, it was ranked as a {{ill|lt=Modern system of ranked Shinto shrines#Government shrines|Q712534|WD=Q712534|lt=Kanpei-shōsha|1=Modern system of ranked Shinto shrines}}. Today, it is listed as a {{ill|lt=Beppyo Shrine|Q10898274|WD=Q10898274|1=Beppyo shrine}} under the {{ill|lt=Association of Shinto Shrines|Q908077|WD=Q908077|1=Association of Shinto Shrines}}.

In addition to the name “Tomo Gion-gū,” it is also commonly called “Gion-san” or “Ogion-san.”

=== Enshrined deities ===
The shrine enshrines the following two deities<ref>The description of the enshrined deities is based on the [http://tomo-gionsan.com/yuisyo.shtml History] (official website).</ref>.
; Principal deity
:* {{ill|lt=Watatsumi|Q1474187|WD=Q1474187|lt=Ōwatatsumi-no-Mikoto|1=Watatsumi}} – Formerly the enshrined deity of Watasu Shrine.
; Associated deity
:* {{ill|lt=Susanoo|Q272993|WD=Q272993|lt=Susanoo-no-Mikoto|1=Susanoo-no-Mikoto}} – Formerly the enshrined deity of Tomo Gion-gū.

=== History ===
The present Nunakuma Shrine was formed in the {{ill|lt=Meiji period|Q3125096|WD=Q3125096|1=Meiji era}} by the merger of '''Watasu Shrine''' and '''Tomo Gion-gū''', and was renamed “Nunakuma Shrine” in accordance with the listing in the {{ill|lt=Engishiki|Q1342448|WD=Q1342448|1=Engishiki}} {{ill|lt=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|Q11064932|WD=Q11064932|lt=Register of Deities|1=Engishiki Jinmyōchō}}<ref name="地名">“Place Names of Hiroshima Prefecture,” entry on Nunakuma Shrine.</ref>. The shrine maintains that Watasu Shrine was the shrine listed in the Engishiki and that it continues to the present day<ref name="由緒"/>. The current two enshrined deities ({{ill|lt=Watatsumi|Q1474187|WD=Q1474187|lt=Ōwatatsumi-no-Mikoto|1=Watatsumi}} and {{ill|lt=Susanoo|Q272993|WD=Q272993|lt=Susanoo-no-Mikoto|1=Susanoo-no-Mikoto}}) were formerly the deities of the respective shrines prior to the merger<ref name="由緒">[http://tomo-gionsan.com/yuisyo.shtml History] (official website).</ref>.

=== Watasu Shrine ===
[[File:Nunakuma-jinja Watasu-sha-2.jpg|thumb|200px|right|{{center|Watasu Shrine}}{{small|Former shrine building prior to enshrinement in the main hall.}}]]
According to shrine tradition, the founding of Watasu Shrine (now Nunakuma Shrine) dates to Year 2 of Emperor {{ill|lt=Chūai|Q179971|WD=Q179971|1=Emperor Chūai}}, when {{ill|lt=Empress Jingū|Q232803|WD=Q232803|1=Empress Jingū}} prayed for safe sea passage by enshrining Watatsumi-no-Mikoto on a sacred stone at this site while heading westward<ref name="由緒"/>. Upon her return, she offered a “Izuno Takatomo” (a type of bow accessory; {{ill|lt=Tomo|Q11663860|WD=Q11663860|1=tomo}} refers to one such implement), from which the place name “Tomo” is said to have originated<ref name="由緒"/>.

In the mid-{{ill|lt=Heian period|Q193292|WD=Q193292|1=Heian period}}, the {{ill|lt=Engishiki|Q1342448|WD=Q1342448|1=Engishiki}} {{ill|lt=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|Q11064932|WD=Q11064932|lt=Register of Deities|1=Engishiki Jinmyōchō}} lists a {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Shikinaisha|1=Shikinaisha|WD=Q134917286}} named “Nunakuma Shrine” in {{ill|lt=Bingo Province|Q1049113|WD=Q1049113|1=Bingo Province}}, {{ill|lt=Numakuma District|Q7069042|WD=Q7069042|1=Numakuma District, Hiroshima}}. The reading is given as either “Nunasaki” or “Nunakuma.” Later, the location of this shrine became unknown<ref name="地名"/>. In early modern scholarship, it is identified with Watasu Shrine, though whether it was located in Tomo remains unclear<ref name="地名"/>.

In the early modern period, Watasu Shrine is said to have enshrined Sarutahiko-no-Kami and Funadama-no-Mikoto (also called Funatama-no-Mikoto)<ref name="地名"/>. It is also said to have originally been located either in Ushiroji-Hiramura or Tomo-no-Sekimachi, and to have been relocated to its present site as a subsidiary shrine of the Gion Shrine during the {{ill|lt=Keichō|Q1328185|WD=Q1328185|1=Keichō}} era ({{ill|lt=1596|Q6785|WD=Q6785|1=1596}}–{{ill|lt=1615|Q6784|WD=Q6784|1=1615}})<ref name="地名"/>. During the {{ill|lt=Meireki|Q1192766|WD=Q1192766|1=Meireki}} era ({{ill|lt=1655|Q6975|WD=Q6975|1=1655}}–{{ill|lt=1658|Q6991|WD=Q6991|1=1658}}), the shrine buildings were rebuilt by the domain lord {{ill|lt=Mizuno Katsusada|Q10565395|WD=Q10565395|1=Mizuno Katsusada}}<ref name="地名"/><ref>“Gods of Japan” states that Watasu Shrine was originally in Nishi-machi, Tomo, but after being destroyed by fire in Keichō 4 (1599), it was relocated to Asadani in Ushiroji Village, and later moved to the precincts of the Gion Shrine when it was rebuilt in Jōkyō 2 (1674).</ref>.

=== Tomo Gion-gū ===
According to the shrine, the founding date of Tomo Gion-gū is unknown<ref name="由緒"/>. It is variously said to have been founded during the {{ill|lt=Tenchō|Q1373138|WD=Q1373138|1=Tenchō}} era ({{ill|lt=824|Q30353|WD=Q30353|1=824}}–{{ill|lt=834|Q30381|WD=Q30381|1=834}}) or established by invitation during the {{ill|lt=Hōgen|Q1193898|WD=Q1193898|1=Hōgen (era)}} era ({{ill|lt=1156|Q19653|WD=Q19653|1=1156}}–{{ill|lt=1159|Q19659|WD=Q19659|1=1159}})<ref name="地名"/>.

The enshrined deity was, as now, {{ill|lt=Susanoo|Q272993|WD=Q272993|lt=Susanoo-no-Mikoto|1=Susanoo-no-Mikoto}}<ref name="地名"/>. The shrine was originally located in Sekimachi, Tomo, but was destroyed by fire in {{ill|lt=Keichō|Q1328185|WD=Q1328185|1=Keichō}} 4 ({{ill|lt=1599|Q6803|WD=Q6803|1=1599}}) and subsequently relocated to its present site<ref name="神々">“Gods of Japan,” entry on Nunakuma Shrine.</ref>.

There is also a theory that this Tomo Gion-gū corresponds to the “{{ill|lt=Ekuma no Kunitsuyashiro|UNKNOWN|lt=Ekumi no Kunitsuyashiro}}” mentioned in fragments of the {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Bingo Province Fudoki|1=UNKNOWN}}<ref name="地名"/>. The shrine was revered as the tutelary deity of Tomo and received many donations from the lords of the Fukuyama Domain, owning shrine lands as well<ref name="地名"/>.

=== Both shrines from the early modern period onward ===
The subsequent history of both shrines is as follows<ref name="神々"/>:
* During the Edo period, Tomo Gion-gū stood at this site, with Watasu Shrine as a subsidiary shrine within its precincts.
* In {{ill|lt=Meiji period|Q3125096|WD=Q3125096|1=Meiji era}} 1 ({{ill|lt=1868|Q7717|WD=Q7717|1=1868}}), Tomo Gion-gū was renamed “Susanoo Shrine.”
* In Meiji 4 ({{ill|lt=1871|Q7746|WD=Q7746|1=1871}}), this Susanoo Shrine was designated as the Shikinaisha, renamed “Nunakuma Shrine,” and ranked as a {{ill|lt=Modern system of ranked Shinto shrines#Government shrines|Q712534|WD=Q712534|lt=Kanpei-shōsha|1=Modern system of ranked Shinto shrines}} under the {{ill|lt=Modern system of ranked Shinto shrines|Q712534|WD=Q712534|1=Modern system of ranked Shinto shrines}}.
* In {{ill|lt=1875|Q7764|WD=Q7764|1=1875}} (Meiji 8) May, it was revised that Watasu Shrine was the Shikinaisha.
* In {{ill|lt=1876|Q7766|WD=Q7766|1=1876}} (Meiji 9) July, the spirit of Watasu Shrine was enshrined in the main hall, and the Gion deity was moved to an associated position (reversal of principal and subordinate). The Funadama deity of Watasu Shrine was reidentified as Ōwatatsumi-no-Mikoto.

=== After World War II ===
* In {{ill|lt=1957|Q5311|WD=Q5311|1=1957}} (Shōwa 32) May, a main hall relocation ceremony was conducted. Offerings of ritual offerings and sacred food were presented by {{ill|lt=Emperor Shōwa|Q34479|WD=Q34479|1=Hirohito}}<ref>{{Cite book |ja |author=Imperial Household Agency |title=The Shōwa Emperor’s Veritable Records, Vol. 12 |publisher=Tokyo Shoseki |date=2017-03-28 |page=327 |isbn=978-4-487-74412-1}}</ref>.

=== Shrine precincts ===
[[File:Nunakuma-jinja shaden-2.jpg|thumb|220px|right|{{center|Shrine buildings}}]]
[[File:Nunakuma-jinja butai.JPG|thumb|220px|right|{{center|Noh stage (National {{ill|lt=Important Cultural Property|Q1188622|WD=Q1188622|1=Important Cultural Property (Japan)}})}}]]
The '''stone lanterns''' standing in front of the shrine buildings were donated in {{ill|lt=Keian|Q1146135|WD=Q1146135|1=Keian}} 4 ({{ill|lt=1651|Q6956|WD=Q6956|1=1651}}) of the {{ill|lt=Edo period|Q184963|WD=Q184963|1=Edo period}} by the third lord of the Fukuyama Domain, {{ill|lt=Mizuno Katsusada|Q10565395|WD=Q10565395|1=Mizuno Katsusada}}<ref name="石燈籠"/>. They stand 3.24 meters tall<ref name="石燈籠"/>. The large lanterns consist of a hexagonal base, a shaft stone 47.4 cm in diameter, and a middle platform, fire box, roof stone, and finial<ref name="石燈籠"/>. As exemplary shrine-front lanterns of the early modern period, they are designated Important Cultural Properties of Fukuyama City<ref name="石燈籠"/>.

Next to the Hachiman Shrine within the precincts are 20 '''chikara-ishi''' (strength stones). All are made of {{ill|lt=granite|Q41177|WD=Q41177|1=Granite}}, oval in shape, and weigh between 118 and 230 kilograms<ref name="力石"/>. Each stone bears an inscription; five with known dates range from {{ill|lt=Tenpō|Q1369045|WD=Q1369045|1=Tenpō}} 15 ({{ill|lt=1844|Q7633|WD=Q7633|1=1844}}) to {{ill|lt=Ansei|Q1327259|WD=Q1327259|1=Ansei}} 5 ({{ill|lt=1858|Q7684|WD=Q7684|1=1858}}). As Tomo was a maritime town, these stones are thought to have been offered in strength contests during festivals<ref name="力石"/>. Together with three stones at a nearby Sumiyoshi Shrine, they are designated as the “Tomo-no-Tsu Chikara-ishi,” Tangible Folk Cultural Properties of Fukuyama City<ref name="力石"/>.

One level below the main shrine buildings stands a Momoyama-period '''Noh stage'''. The structure is one bay by one bay, single-roofed, gabled, tsumairi style, with shingle roofing. It is said to have originally been a portable stage within {{ill|lt=Fushimi Castle|Q1196273|WD=Q1196273|1=Fushimi Castle}}, favored by {{ill|lt=Toyotomi Hideyoshi|Q187550|WD=Q187550|1=Toyotomi Hideyoshi}}, and later granted by the second shogun {{ill|lt=Tokugawa Hidetada|Q314464|WD=Q314464|1=Tokugawa Hidetada}} to {{ill|lt=Mizuno Katsunari|Q8190277|WD=Q8190277|1=Mizuno Katsushige}}, the first lord of the Fukuyama Domain, along with the Fushimi Yagura of Fukuyama Castle<ref name="説明板"/><ref name="能舞台"/>. During the {{ill|lt=Manji|Q1145295|WD=Q1145295|1=Manji (era)}} era ({{ill|lt=1658|Q6991|WD=Q6991|1=1658}}–{{ill|lt=1660|Q7001|WD=Q7001|1=1660}}), the third lord {{ill|lt=Mizuno Katsusada|Q10565395|WD=Q10565395|1=Mizuno Katsusada}} donated it to the shrine, and in {{ill|lt=Genbun|Q1145754|WD=Q1145754|1=Genbun}} 3 ({{ill|lt=1738|Q6996|WD=Q6996|1=1738}}) it was converted into the fixed stage seen today<ref name="能舞台"/>. Features of its portable construction remain, with numbered markings on each component, indicating it could be transported even to battlefields<ref name="説明板"/>. The pine and bamboo painted on the kagami-ita (mirror board) date from the original period<ref name="能舞台"/>. The present bridge and dressing rooms, however, are later additions<ref name="能舞台"/>. Due to its valuable Momoyama-period characteristics, the stage is designated a National Important Cultural Property<ref name="能舞台"/>.

The '''second torii gate''' at the lowest level of the precincts was donated in {{ill|lt=Kan’ei|Q1192913|WD=Q1192913|1=Kan'ei}} 2 ({{ill|lt=1625|Q6826|WD=Q6826|1=1625}}) by Mizuno Katsushige (later the second lord {{ill|lt=Mizuno Katsutoshi|Q10565388|WD=Q10565388|1=Mizuno Katsutoshi}}) upon the birth of his eldest son (later the third lord {{ill|lt=Mizuno Katsusada|Q10565395|WD=Q10565395|1=Mizuno Katsusada}}), praying for his health<ref name="鳥居"/>. Although it is of the common Myōjin torii type, the ends of the kasagi are rounded and upturned, and a torifusuma is added, giving it a distinctive form<ref name="鳥居"/>. The upturned ends resemble the “Hizen torii” style, which may relate to the inscription on the pillars reading “Carpenter: Nakajima Yahyōe of Hizen”<ref name="鳥居"/>. This torii is designated an Important Cultural Property of Hiroshima Prefecture<ref name="鳥居"/>.

