=== Festivals ===
=== Annual festivals ===
* Tsukinami-sai (1st of every month)<ref>The annual festival section is based on [http://tomo-gionsan.com/saiji.shtml Festivals] (official website).</ref>
* Tsukinami-sai (14th of every month)
* New Year’s Day Festival (January 1)
* Kinen-sai (February 17)
* Main Festival (May 2)
* Great Purification Rite (June 30)
* Summer Festival
** Shinkō-sai (2nd Sunday of July)
** Kankō-sai (3rd Sunday of July)
* Autumn Festival (Watasu Shrine Main Festival) (Friday to Sunday before the 3rd Monday of September)
* Niiname-sai (November 23)

=== Special rituals ===
; Oyumi Shinji
: Held on the 2nd Sunday of February. A festival of the Hachiman Shrine, originally conducted on the 7th day of the lunar New Year<ref name="お弓"/>. Participants include one adult parent archer and one adult child archer, two male pages, and two young arrow retrievers<ref name="お弓"/>. After performing ancient rites, arrows are shot from the stage toward a target in front<ref name="お弓"/>. This ritual is said to have evolved from the {{ill|lt=hamaya|Q138804|WD=Q138804|lt=hamayumi|1=Hamaya}} used to ward off evil at the beginning of the year, and is designated an Intangible Folk Cultural Property of the city<ref name="お弓"/>.

; Otebi Shinji
: “Otebi Shinji.” Held on the eve of the 2nd Sunday of July. A purification rite associated with Tomo Gion-gū, preceding the mikoshi procession<ref name="お手火"/>. Formerly held on the 4th day of the lunar 6th month<ref name="お手火"/>. During the ritual, sacred fire is lit on a large torch placed at the Zuishin Gate, and this torch is carried to the worship hall over the course of an hour<ref name="お手火"/>. After the mikoshi is brought out and placed in the worship hall, the torch is paraded through the precincts and town to purify defilement<ref name="お手火"/>. This ritual is designated an Intangible Folk Cultural Property of the city<ref name="お手火"/>.

=== Cultural properties ===
=== Important Cultural Properties (national designation) ===
* Noh Stage (Structure) – Designated November 14, 1953 (Shōwa 28)<ref name="能舞台">{{National Cultural Properties Database|102|3147|Nunakuma Shrine Noh Stage}}<br />[http://www.pref.hiroshima.lg.jp/site/bunkazai/bunkazai-data-102010240.html Nunakuma Shrine Noh Stage] (Hiroshima Prefectural Board of Education).<br />[http://www.city.fukuyama.hiroshima.jp/soshiki/bunka/5155.html Nunakuma Shrine Noh Stage] (Fukuyama City website).</ref>.

=== Hiroshima Prefecture–designated cultural properties ===
* Important Cultural Property (Tangible Cultural Property)
** Torii Gate (Structure) – Early Edo period, built in Kan’ei 2 (1625). Designated February 5, 1957 (Shōwa 32)<ref name="鳥居">[http://www.pref.hiroshima.lg.jp/site/bunkazai/bunkazai-data-202010110.html Nunakuma Shrine Torii] (Hiroshima Prefectural Board of Education).<br />[http://www.city.fukuyama.hiroshima.jp/soshiki/bunka/5156.html Nunakuma Shrine Torii] (Fukuyama City website).</ref>.

=== Fukuyama City–designated cultural properties ===
* Important Cultural Property (Tangible Cultural Property)
** Pair of Stone Lanterns (Structure)
**: Early Edo period, built in Keian 4 (1651). Designated January 31, 1967 (Shōwa 42)<ref name="石燈籠">[http://www.city.fukuyama.hiroshima.jp/soshiki/bunka/5143.html Nunakuma Shrine Stone Lanterns] (Fukuyama City website).</ref>.
** Noh Masks and Boxes (Crafts)
**: All from the Momoyama period and donated by {{ill|lt=Mizuno Katsunari|Q8190277|WD=Q8190277|1=Mizuno Katsushige}}, the first lord of the Fukuyama Domain. They are said to have originally been prized items of Toyotomi Hideyoshi, received by Katsunari from Tokugawa Hidetada together with the Noh stage. The Okina mask measures 19.39 cm in height and 16.06 cm in width, showing Momoyama-period stylistic features. The mask box is a reused item, decorated with gold maki-e on black lacquer depicting Ming-dynasty Chinese genre scenes. The outer box was made to preserve the mask box; an ink inscription inside the lid indicates it was prepared in {{ill|lt=Eiroku|Q1143341|WD=Q1143341|1=Eiroku}} 8 ({{ill|lt=1565|Q6634|WD=Q6634|1=1565}}) by the Nakasuga family, hereditary shrine priests. Designated January 31, 1967 (Shōwa 42)<ref>[http://www.city.fukuyama.hiroshima.jp/soshiki/bunka/5228.html Noh Masks and Boxes] (Fukuyama City website).</ref>.
** Chigusa-made Kagura Tube (Craft)
**: Donated in {{ill|lt=Manji|Q1145295|WD=Q1145295|1=Manji (era)}} 3 ({{ill|lt=1660|Q7001|WD=Q7001|1=1660}}) by {{ill|lt=Mizuno Katsusada|Q10565395|WD=Q10565395|1=Mizuno Katsusada}}, the third lord of the Fukuyama Domain, together with the Noh stage. The maker, Chigusa, was a Muromachi-period Noh mask carver. The tube is made of paulownia wood with black lacquer and gold maki-e phoenix designs. Designated January 31, 1967 (Shōwa 42)<ref>[http://www.city.fukuyama.hiroshima.jp/soshiki/bunka/5227.html Chigusa-made Kagura Tube] (Fukuyama City website).</ref>.
* Tangible Folk Cultural Property
** Tomo-no-Tsu Strength Stones – Designated together with 3 stones from Sumiyoshi Shrine. Designated September 22, 2000 (Heisei 12)<ref name="力石">[http://www.city.fukuyama.hiroshima.jp/soshiki/bunka/5271.html Tomo-no-Tsu Strength Stones] (Fukuyama City website).</ref>.
* Intangible Folk Cultural Property
** Oyumi Shinji – Designated March 31, 1971 (Shōwa 46)<ref name="お弓">[http://www.city.fukuyama.hiroshima.jp/soshiki/bunka/5264.html Oyumi Shinji] (Fukuyama City website).</ref>.
** Otebi Shinji – Designated March 31, 1973 (Shōwa 48)<ref name="お手火">[http://www.city.fukuyama.hiroshima.jp/soshiki/bunka/5265.html Otebi Shinji] (Fukuyama City website).</ref>.

